# Tracer: beta-reduced anonymous applications use structural equality replay.
#
# Before (did not work):
# forall f, g fn(x R) R, a R:
#     forall x R:
#         f(x) = f(-x)
#         g(x) = g(-x)
#     =>:
#         f(a) = f(-a)
#         g(a) = g(-a)
#         fn(x R) R {f(x) * g(x)}(a) = fn(x R) R {f(x) * g(x)}(-a)
# Former behavior: the last equality was unknown unless the beta-reduced
# product equality was written out as an intermediate conclusion.
#
# Now (verified):
forall f, g fn(x R) R, a R:
    forall x R:
        f(x) = f(-x)
        g(x) = g(-x)
    =>:
        f(a) = f(-a)
        g(a) = g(-a)
        fn(x R) R {f(x) * g(x)}(a) = fn(x R) R {f(x) * g(x)}(-a)
# Current behavior: both complete anonymous applications beta-reduce, then
# multiplication congruence closes its two leaves from the stored equalities.
# Boundary: beta reduction does not instantiate the known forall or invent a
# missing factor equality; the executable Rust regression omits g(a) = g(b).
# Evidence: target/release/litex -compact -isolated -runner -f examples/01_proof_patterns/anonymous_function_beta_structural_equality.lit
# Implementation: src/runtime/runtime_known_object_properties.rs and src/verify/verify_builtin_rules/equality_structural.rs
